The BlackCAT CubeSat is a coded-aperture X-ray telescope mission designed to monitor the sky for transient photon bursts, such as Gamma-Ray Bursts and X-ray flares. Operating across multiple energy bands from 0.5 to 20.0 keV, the instrument aims to detect and monitor the physical processes of hot plasmas, accretion disks, and high-energy coronae in deep-space sources like Scorpius X-1 (SCO-X1). As a coded-mask imaging system, BlackCAT provides wide-field observation capabilities, but its raw telemetry requires sophisticated mathematical reconstruction to map photon arrivals into actionable sky images. This paper presents the development of a semi-automated software pipeline designed to convert raw event-list telemetry into human readable data and images. We detail the implementation of geometric effective area corrections, source-detection weighting algorithms, and orbital telemetry analysis. Our results demonstrate the successful transformation of raw satellite data into flux-calibrated light curves and sky maps, providing a robust framework for long-term monitoring of high-energy sources such as Scorpius X-1 (Sco X-1).

